{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1\fmodern Courier;}}\fs22 {\b\fs32 Niche Hunter - Google Ads API Design Documentation}\par Basic access application. Prepared 2026-08-29.\par \par {\b Company Name}: Niche Hunter. This is not a company. It is a personal, non-commercial research project run by one individual. Open source at https://github.com/julio-arcila/niche-hunter\par \par {\b Business Model}: There is no business model. The project has no revenue, no customers, no clients and no advertising spend. It does not sell, resell, redistribute or expose Google Ads data in any form. It exists to answer one question for its author: which content topics have audience demand that existing video content does not already serve.\par \par {\b Tool Access/Use}: The tool is a command-line application run by its author on a personal machine. There is no web interface, no hosted service, no dashboard and no login. No other person can invoke it, and no third party supplies or uses their own Google Ads credentials through it. It manages no advertising accounts for anyone. Reports it produces are plain text files on the author's machine; any figures shared with others are aggregated research findings, never Google Ads data in raw or reconstructable form.\par \par {\b Tool Design}: The tool is a nightly Python pipeline. Each data source is a separate collector with the same contract: a fetch step that performs network I/O and stores the response verbatim, and a pure normalize step that converts it into typed rows. Collected values land in a local SQLite database as append-only daily snapshots, and metrics are computed from those snapshots.\par Sources already in use, all free and public: the YouTube Data API for what content already exists; public YouTube RSS feeds for upload cadence; the Wikimedia Pageview API for topic-level audience attention; and Google Trends for the shape of search interest over time.\par The Google Ads API fills exactly one gap: those sources give no absolute search volume and no measure of advertiser value. Keyword Planner supplies both. The tool reads historical monthly search volumes and top-of-page bid estimates for a fixed, hand-curated list of roughly 70 topic keywords, one call per market, refreshed no more than monthly because the underlying figures are twelve-month averages that do not change daily. Results are cached for seven days. Expected volume is on the order of 100 requests per month against a single manager account (375-041-4091).\par The integration is strictly read-only. The tool issues no mutate operations of any kind. It creates, modifies and pauses nothing; it uploads no conversions; it builds no audiences; it runs no campaigns. Until now the same data was obtained by manually downloading the Keyword Planner CSV export from the web interface. API access replaces that manual step, and adds the twelve monthly data points that the CSV export leaves empty.\par \par {\b API Services Called}:\par    -  KeywordPlanIdeaService.GenerateKeywordHistoricalMetrics. This is the only method called. It retrieves avg_monthly_searches, monthly_search_volumes, competition, competition_index and top-of-page bid low/high for a supplied keyword list, scoped to one geo target constant and one language constant.\par No other service is used. Specifically, the tool does not use the App Conversion Tracking and Remarketing API, ConversionUploadService, campaign or ad group services, or any audience or remarketing service.\par \par {\b Tool Output}: The tool is not externally accessible, so there is no user interface to mock up. Its interface is a command line. Actual output from the installed tool:\par \par {\f1\fs18 SOURCE            PORTED   CONFIGURED   QUOTA    PROTOTYPE}\par {\f1\fs18 youtube_api       yes      yes          9500     legacy/niche_hunter_yt.py}\par {\f1\fs18 youtube_rss       yes      yes          -        legacy/niche_hunter_rss.py}\par {\f1\fs18 wikipedia         yes      yes          -        (none)}\par {\f1\fs18 trends            yes      yes          -        legacy/niche_hunter_trends.py}\par {\f1\fs18 keyword_planner   yes      yes          -        legacy/niche_hunter_kp.py}\par \par A keyword import reports what it stored, for example: status ok, raw payloads 67, keyword rows 66 new, matched a seed term 66/66. Stored rows carry the source, the run identifier, the observation timestamp, the geo and the currency exactly as the API returned them. No currency conversion is applied and no value is imputed.\par \par {\b Data Handling}: Google Ads data is stored only in a local database on the author's machine, is never transmitted to any third party, and is never published. The repository is public, but it contains only source code: credentials are excluded from version control, and no Google Ads data is committed to it.\par }